AB  - This paper presents a comprehensive review of the current landscape of artificial intelligence (AI) applications in sign language education for deaf and hard-of-hearing (DHH) students, with a special focus on low-resource languages such as Palestinian Sign Language (PSL). It analyzes recent advancements across three primary domains: sign language recognition (SLR) systems powered by deep learning and computer vision, adaptive tutoring solutions utilizing large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT, and immersive educational platforms enabled by augmented reality (AR). While AI tools have shown promising potential, the study identifies critical gaps in existing systems, including limited real-time interaction, a lack of STEM curriculum integration, and minimal adaptation to the cultural-linguistic context of PSL. In response, this paper proposes an integrated AI-powered educational framework that combines real-time PSL recognition, intelligent dialogue generation, and STEM-focused content delivery. The proposed approach aims to bridge the educational and linguistic divide for DHH learners and serves as a scalable model for inclusive education in low-resource and multilingual settings. © 2025 IEEE.
