AB  - Non-rigid 3D-to-2D registration is a crucial yet challenging task for augmented reality-guided laparoscopic liver surgery, hindered by significant tissue deformation, data scarcity, and a lack of physical constraints in existing methods. We propose a novel, hybrid-driven, two-stage framework to address these challenges. Our method first establishes a robust rigid alignment using a differentiable renderer. Subsequently, a new Transformer-based architecture models the complex non-rigid deformation. This network first uses a Multi-Head Squeeze-and-Excitation module for adaptive feature recalibration, followed by a landmark-guided cross-attention mechanism to fuse sparse anatomical semantics into the dense point cloud representation. Critically, we introduce Position-Based Dynamics to ensure the biomechanical plausibility of the deformation. We utilize large-scale 3D generative model that provides rich feature priors and alleviates data scarcity. Extensive experiments show that our synergistic approach achieves superior registration accuracy, paving the way for more reliable clinical surgical navigation. © 2025 IEEE.
