TI  - The Use of Virtual Reality to Develop Presentation Skills in Entrepreneurship Education: A Systematic Review of the Literature and Select Commercial Technologies
PY  - 2025
T2  - Journal of Interactive Learning Research
VL  - 36
IS  - 2
SP  - 157
EP  - 189
DO  - 10.70725/822417qrvihu
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105006718748?origin=resultslist
AB  - Development of effective oral presentation skills is an essential component of entrepreneurship education, but class size and time constraints can limit opportunities to practice and receive quality feedback. Immersive virtual reality (VR) can provide time-and place-independent opportunities to hone presentation skills in diverse settings with personalized feedback. Focusing specifically on research carried out in the last ten years, this PRISMA-guided systematic literature review aimed to assess the kinds of VR, learning conditions, forms of feedback, and commercial technologies best able to support presentation skill development in entrepreneurs, as well as key areas for future research. Fully immersive VR had the greatest positive impact on presentation skill development. Effectiveness was enhanced by technology familiarization, pretraining, segmentation, repetition, and integration into a program of learning. Peer feedback, self-assessment, and both computer-and instructor-mediated feedback were found to be effective as long as they were consis-tent and supported experiential learning. Five commercial virtual reality applications referenced in peer-reviewed literature were compared to help instructors determine what technologies might meet the needs of their learners. Future research focusing on in-structor-independent interventions and assessing how generative AI can best support VR-based learning would benefit the field. © 2025, Association for the Advancement of Computing in Education. All rights reserved.
