AB  - Precise modeling of virtual reality art perception is a core challenge in digital art research. Traditional perception assessment methods struggle to capture users' complex multi-dimensional perceptual states in VR environments. This study proposes a VR art perception computing framework that integrates embodied phenomenology theory with multimodal deep learning. The framework achieves accurate quantification and prediction of user artistic experiences by constructing embodied perceptual state spaces, designing cross-modal spatiotemporal attention mechanisms, and establishing dynamic perception assessment models. Experimental validation on large-scale VR art datasets shows that the proposed framework significantly outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods in both artistic emotion recognition and perceptual immersion prediction tasks, with relative performance improvements exceeding 15%. Ablation experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of phenomenology-guided feature representation and spatiotemporal attention mechanisms. The framework provides theoretical foundation and technical support for VR art creation optimization, personalized recommendation, and user experience evaluation, promoting theoretical development and practical applications in digital art perception computing.
