AB  - Existing electroencephalography (EEG)-based virtual reality motion sickness (VRMS) prediction methods often struggle to capture comprehensive spatiotemporal dependencies and maintain cross-individual generalization. This study proposes a dual-branch Transformer-generative adversarial network (DBT-GAN) framework that integrates local convolutional neural network - long short-term memory (CNN-LSTM) representations with global transformer dependencies and introduces a tri-attentive mechanism across temporal, spatial, and frequency dimensions. The generative adversarial network (GAN) generator alleviates data scarcity through realistic EEG synthesis, while the discriminator improves data integrity via anomaly detection, forming a closed-loop quality control system. Evaluations on public and cross-domain datasets confirm that the proposed model achieves state-of-the-art predictive accuracy, superior generalization, and enhanced robustness under data imbalance. Furthermore, the model's learned feature patterns exhibit strong consistency with questionnaire-based susceptibility assessments, demonstrating both predictive reliability and physiological interpretability. These findings highlight the framework's potential for practical applications in real-time virtual reality (VR) motion-sickness monitoring and personalized intervention. © 2026 IEEE.
