SP  - 1581
EP  - 1585
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821159
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3821159
AB  - Against the backdrop of globalization and digitalization, the transmission of Intangible Cultural Heritage (ICH) faces significant challenges, such as diminishing cultural identity and insufficient user engagement. Existing cultural and creative designs often remain confined to the superficial level of symbolic translation, lacking a systematic mechanism capable of effectively fostering cultural identity. Drawing upon the “Cognition-Affect-Behavior” (C-A-B) model of cultural identity, this paper proposes a digital design methodology specifically tailored for ICH-inspired cultural and creative products. First, this methodology achieves a structured representation of ICH information through the extraction and encoding of cultural elements; second, it establishes a mapping mechanism between the three-tiered model of cultural identity and specific design elements. Furthermore, by integrating parametric design and digital generative technologies, the study constructs a design workflow framework encompassing the stages of “data acquisition—feature translation-generative design-user feedback.” The efficacy of this methodology is validated through a case study involving ICH-inspired apparel design, utilizing user questionnaires to assess users' cognitive understanding, emotional resonance, and behavioral intentions. The research findings demonstrate that this methodology effectively enhances users' level of cultural identity, offering a novel methodological pathway and practical reference for the design of ICH-related cultural and creative products.
