TI  - A Real-Time Rendering Algorithm for Dongguan Water Ripple Dynamic Art Installation Integrating Reaction-Diffusion Equations and GAN
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 930
EP  - 935
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821060
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3821060
AB  - Immersive art installations increasingly demand real-time, high-fidelity, and artistically controllable fluid rendering. However, traditional physics-based simulations often lack artistic stylization, while standard generative adversarial networks (GANs) suffer from severe temporal instability and a lack of physical constraints in video generation. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a novel Reaction-Diffusion conditional GAN (RD-cGAN) fusion framework for real-time, emotion-driven water ripple generation. Our method leverages the Reaction-Diffusion (RD) equation to compute a physical structure tensor, providing a stable dynamic baseline, which is then fed into an emotion-labeled cGAN for artistic stylization. To ensure inter-frame smoothness, a temporal consistency loss is integrated into the training pipeline. The model is trained on a custom dataset comprising 15,000 high-resolution frames captured from the real-world water towns of Dongguan, ensuring regional cultural authenticity. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed RD-cGAN achieves a Frechet Inception Distance (FID) of 14.2 and a Temporal Error of 0.035, significantly outperforming baseline models in both visual fidelity and temporal stability. Furthermore, optimized with TensorRT half-precision inference, the system achieves a stable real-time rendering performance of 65 FPS at 4K resolution. The proposed framework successfully bridges physical realism with artistic expression, providing a robust and highly interactive solution for large-scale digital cultural exhibitions.
