AB  - This chapter examines how the convergence of Extended Reality (XR) and generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) reshapes the institutional capacity-building imperatives established in Section III of this volume. Building on Chapter 9’s five-dimensional framework for XR adoption (Content, Institutional Culture, Implementation and System Operations, Financial Resources, Technology Infrastructure), Chapter 10’s producer competency development through systematic VR design curricula, and Chapter 11’s consumer competency training for faculty integrating existing platforms, the chapter demonstrates that AI integration introduces new complexity across each established dimension while offering expanded capabilities for personalization, adaptability, and accessibility when deployed with pedagogical intent and ethical oversight. The analysis extends Chapter 9’s maturity phase framework (Experimental, Tactical, Scaled) to AI/XR contexts, clarifies how AI blurs the producer-consumer competency boundary requiring updated workforce and faculty development (Chapters 10–11), and elevates governance from peripheral concern to central capacity-building imperative. Grounded in constructivist, constructionist, and experiential learning traditions, the chapter synthesizes research on immersive learning mechanisms (immersion, presence, agency, embodiment) and AI-enabled educational capabilities (adaptive feedback, intelligent tutoring, dynamic scenarios, accessibility supports) to articulate conditions under which AI/XR convergence supports learning objectives rather than merely adding technological complexity. The chapter proposes capacity-building frameworks organized around two recurring imperatives: instructional integrity (alignment to learning objectives, valid assessment, cognitive scaffolding, and faculty expertise preservation) and stewardship (privacy protection, equity promotion, algorithmic transparency, and institutional accountability). Practical guidance addresses how AI affects each of Chapter 9’s five dimensions, how institutions can apply phased progression to avoid premature scaling, what governance mechanisms operationalize integrity and stewardship commitments, and how procurement, faculty development, and cross-functional collaboration enable responsible AI/XR adoption. The chapter concludes with implications for building institutional capacity that is technologically advanced, pedagogically sound, ethically governed, and explicitly equity-oriented, alongside research directions examining empirical outcomes, longitudinal effects, governance effectiveness, and equity impacts of AI-enhanced immersive learning. © 2027 selection and editorial matter, Daria Mizza; individual chapters, the contributors.
