AB  - As Augmented Reality (AR) glasses transition into everyday wearables, design must shift from technical utility to psychological thriving. However, a “design-to-development gap” hinders the creation of interactions that foster users' intrinsic motivation. This paper presents a co-creative inquiry integrating the Motivation, Engagement, and Thriving in User Experience (METUX) model into the AR lifecycle. Through a co-design process with five multidisciplinary teams (N = 22), we explored how AR technologies can support well-being in physical activities. Using speculative prototyping and participatory reflection, we synthesize three core design principles, Foundational Competence, Meaningful Choice, and Shared Experience, as actionable levers for human-centered AR. Moving beyond high-fidelity system implementation, this work offers design-led insights into shaping AR systems that respect user autonomy. We conclude with design implications for using psychological frameworks as generative tools to bridge conceptual design and future AR development, prioritizing long-term human thriving in emerging immersive systems. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
