AB  - The increasing use of large language models (LLMs) raises concerns regarding energy consumption and environmental impact, yet awareness among end users remains limited. This paper presents Carbon Chat VR, a virtual reality serious game designed to introduce primary school learners to the environmental implications of AI usage through embodied interaction and gamified learning. The system situates players in a virtual classroom where access to a simulated chatbot is controlled through resource-gated mechanics, linking AI queries to visible environmental consequences. The application was developed using a structured serious-game methodology and theoretically validated through established educational frameworks, including ICAP, Self-Regulated Learning, 4E Cognition, and Flow Theory. Exploratory play-testing incorporated spatial movement tracking to analyse user navigation and engagement within the environment. Results demonstrate the feasibility of using immersive VR experiences to communicate abstract sustainability concepts and support classroom discussion. Carbon Chat VR contributes a novel approach to Green AI awareness by translating invisible computational costs into tangible interactive experiences. © 2026 IEEE.
