TI  - CLIP-ViT Enhanced Multimodal Framework for Artistic Conception Understanding and Modeling Correlation Analysis in Chinese Freehand Figures
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 262
EP  - 267
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3820955
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3820955
AB  - General-purpose vision-language models frequently exhibit domain maladaptation when applied to traditional Chinese art, primarily due to the profound semantic gap between abstract artistic conceptions (Yijing) and concrete brushwork modeling (Zaoxing). To address this challenge, this paper proposes an enhanced CLIP-ViT multimodal analysis framework specifically designed for computational art history. The proposed architecture integrates three core components: a classical poetry-aware text encoder customized to parse the dense metaphorical imagery of traditional inscriptions, a knowledge graph-based semantic enhancement module that injects external cultural priors to mitigate data sparsity, and a bidirectional cross-attention mechanism that explicitly aligns textual conceptions with localized visual patches. To evaluate the framework, we constructed a highly curated dataset comprising 350 multimodal pairs categorized into six core aesthetic classes. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves a state-of-the-art classification accuracy of 87.3%, significantly outperforming the standard baseline. Furthermore, cross-modal attention visualizations confirm the model's interpretability, proving its capability to accurately map abstract textual aesthetics to specific traditional visual techniques, such as reductive brushwork (Jianbi) and gossamer-like lines (Yousimiao). This research provides a highly interpretable, multimodal deep learning approach for the digital preservation and semantic analysis of cultural heritage.
