PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3799825.3818767
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3799825.3818767
AB  - Effective post-stroke and traumatic brain injury (TBI) rehabilitation demands repetitive, task-specific overground locomotion while managing fall risk and sustaining patient engagement. These requirements are poorly served by extended reality (XR) systems that rely on seated interaction, treadmill-based motion, or artificial locomotion modalities that introduce visuovestibular mismatch and cybersickness. We present a mixed reality (MR) rehabilitation framework integrating immersive overground interaction with a dynamic body weight support (DBWS) system , enabling controller-free full-body locomotion. A large-language-model (LLM) based closed-loop decision-support layer analyzes structured post-task telemetry to generate continuous difficulty multipliers across subsequent task parameters, replacing coarse difficulty tier switching. We describe the system architecture, AI-driven adaptation pipeline, and preliminary patient outcomes from early deployment at Sunnyview Rehabilitation Hospital.
