AB  - Immersive narrative VR can foster children’s exploratory learning in ways that traditional media cannot. However, children’s natural tendency to explore may clash with rigid, scripted narratives, and little is known about how to leverage AI-driven adaptive storytelling to sustain children’s attention, engagement, and emotion in VR. In this paper, we present KidScope, an immersive VR system for 6–10 year-olds that uses Large Language Models (LLMs) to drive an NPC character with adaptive dialogue, complementing a digital picture-book narrative. In two complementary stories, a child helps a lost star find its way home or rescues a sneezing giant in a garden. Each child experiences both a Scripted condition and an LLM condition while wearing eye-tracking glasses; parents observe the sessions. We analyze gaze allocation (AOIs), behavioral/emotional coding, self-report valence ratings, and parent interviews. Results show that under the LLM-driven condition, children look more at the NPC character and less at off-task background, and behaviorally engage in more NPC-centered social interaction. Children’s mood valence increased significantly after the LLM-driven story whereas it did not change in the Scripted condition. Parents reported that children appeared more “invested,” “excited,” and “focused” with the adaptive NPC, but also expressed concerns about dependency and content consistency. Together, these findings suggest that LLM-enhanced adaptive narration can act as a contextual scaffold, redistributing children’s attention toward social interaction while maintaining task goals. Design implications and future work on parent-mediated AI-driven story experiences for children are discussed. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
