AB  - The rapid popularity of artificial intelligence (AI) agents has introduced significant environmental concerns, specifically regarding the energy consumption and carbon emissions generated during inference. Despite this, end-users often lack visibility into the ecological footprint of their digital interactions. We present LOCOMO MR, a Mixed Reality system developed in Unity for Meta Quest 3 that bridges the gap between AI utility and environmental awareness. The system employs a multimodal interface that integrates computer vision and speech recognition, enabling users to query an AI agent about their immediate surroundings. The system calculates and displays carbon emission estimates for each AI inference directly within the user’s field of view, using a graphical metaphor of a speedometer. To assess the system’s efficacy and usability, we conducted a user study evaluating the prototype through the System Usability Scale and semi-structured post-study interviews. Our findings indicate that visualization on the screen can help raise user awareness of the hidden environmental costs of AI. By contextualizing the ecological impact of computation within an immersive MR environment, LOCOMO MR provides a scalable model for promoting sustainable and responsible AI consumption on consumer-grade hardware. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
