AB  - When users want to ask an AI assistant about their surroundings with AR glasses, conventional input methods such as voice input or mid-air gestures can be conspicuous and undesirable in public spaces. This paper proposes LooQuestion, an interaction technique for AI assistants on AR glasses that enables unobtrusive question asking without relying on explicit user input. The system employs a multimodal LLM to generate question candidates from an egocentric image, and the user selects one of the questions displayed in the AR view using head pointing. To design effective prompts, we collected questions from 22 participants responding to egocentric images and clustered them to identify common question types. Without any additional task-specific fine-tuning, the generated question candidates covered 55.0% of human-question clusters on average. We implemented a system on XREAL One using Unity and FastAPI, which presents four candidates at a time and supports regeneration. Our results suggest that a choice-based interaction approach, which eliminates the need for text input or additional hardware, augments situated human inquiry by enabling unobtrusive and socially acceptable question asking for AI assistants in AR. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
