AB  - This work-in-progress paper presents MathVR, a novel extended reality (XR) system designed to support immersive learning with a Large Language Model (LLM)-driven tutor capable of answering questions and explaining concepts. Although recent XR applications enhance visualization, they often lack adaptive conversational guidance. MathVR addresses this gap by enabling multimodal interaction through speech and handwritten sketches. The tutor dynamically projects visual information onto a shared virtual whiteboard through a tagging mechanism, creating synchronized verbal and visual guidance. Unlike traditional digital tutoring systems that provide direct solutions, the conversational agent in MathVR employs guided discovery and scaffolding strategies, encouraging active problem solving. Preliminary user feedback indicates increased engagement and perceived clarity during interaction with the conversational tutor, highlighting the feasibility and perceived usefulness of adaptive multimodal dialogue in immersive learning environments. The goal is to demonstrate how combining VR and LLM-driven interaction can foster engaging learning experiences in mathematics education. © 2026 IEEE.
