UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042670544?origin=resultslist
AB  - With the widespread application of Artificial Intelligence Generated Content (AIGC) in product design, designers can rapidly generate a large number of creative design proposals through keywords (Prompts). However, these massive outputs present a major challenge for product development. Designers struggle to efficiently identify concepts that truly align with enterprise needs from such a large volume of proposals. Therefore, this study proposes a dual-channel design evaluation system that integrates a multimodal cross-attention (MMCA) mechanism and virtual reality (VR) technology, using an automobile cockpit interior (CI) evaluation system as a case study. First, emotion evaluation indicators are constructed based on user affective vocabulary. Their corresponding weights are then calculated using the forward projection area (FPA) method. The MMCA model is then trained to map user emotions to CI design features, enabling the prediction of emotion scores for batches of input images and form codes. A diffusion model (DM) is subsequently employed to generate novel design samples based on large-scale learning from existing CI images, and the MMCA model is used to select preliminary schemes with high emotion scores. Finally, VR technology provides a 360° immersive experience to evaluate these preliminary schemes and determine the final design. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed system can rapidly identify optimal concepts from large volumes of AI-generated designs, reduce designers’ subjective bias and significantly improve the efficiency of moving from concept to realization. Moreover, the framework can be extended to the evaluation of other industrial product designs. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
