AB  - With the rapid development of artificial intelligence in the field of visual creativity, product packaging design is embracing new opportunities for automation and intelligence. However, traditional image generation methods still struggle to meet the complex aesthetic demands, layout structures, and printing compliance requirements of packaging design, often resulting in unstable generation quality and insufficient structural control. This paper proposes an optimization framework for packaging design that integrates image processing techniques with Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs). The framework incorporates structural preservation modules, semantic guidance mechanisms, and color printability constraints to enhance both visual appeal and industrial applicability. A multi-dimensional evaluation metric system was established, and extensive experiments were conducted on typical packaging scenarios such as beverages, personal care, and snacks. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method significantly outperforms mainstream models in terms of aesthetic scores, OCR recognition accuracy, layout consistency, and color compliance. Notably, the model achieved superior performance in ΔE₀₀ color difference control and TAC violation rate. This study not only improves the visual and practical quality of generated designs but also provides a feasible technical path for intelligent packaging design generation.
