AB  - This paper proposes and validates a lightweight framework aimed at utilizing Generative Artificial Intelligence (AIGC) to address the issues of homogenized visual styles and superficial interactive experiences in the digital display of cultural heritage. The core of the framework consists of a visual feature deconstruction model based on painting expertise, a low-threshold stylized translation workflow based on Stable Diffusion and LoRA, and a multimodal interaction prototype system integrated with AIGC real-time generation capability. Using Han Dynasty stone reliefs as an empirical case, this study collects quantitative and qualitative data through a controlled experiment (N=60). The results show that the visual content generated by this method significantly outperforms traditional 3D modeling methods in cultural recognizability (improvement of 42.3%, p<0.01); the developed interaction system also performs better in user experience metrics such as immersion, cultural understanding, and engagement intention. This study provides a feasible low-technical-threshold pathway for researchers with art backgrounds to participate in digital cultural heritage creation. The related process has been successfully applied in digital art course teaching.
