AB  - Supertall building projects employing complex equipment such as Aerial Building Machines involve highly dynamic processes, while traditional representations isolate physical geometry from dynamic process logic. This paper investigates how static geometric models can be computationally transformed into active semantic reasoning engines that help to eliminate cognitive gaps during complex construction operations. The proposed semantic-geometric fusion methodology establishes a dual-dimensional mapping matrix between the Model Breakdown Structure (MBS) and Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) to formalize spatiotemporal dependencies. The resulting Visual Construction Process Model (VCPM), integrated with SWRL-based knowledge graphs, successfully synchronizes 3D entities with temporal nodes to automate constraint reasoning. This framework significantly reduces operational cognitive load and mitigates safety clashes, providing vital decision support for personnel in congested intelligent construction environments. This paradigm inspires future research into integrating domain-specific large language models and probabilistic reasoning for autonomous construction management. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
