TI  - Understanding Prompt Construction and Interaction Challenges in LLM-Based Multi-Robot Systems for Spatial Tasks
PY  - 2026
T2  - Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems - Proceedings
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3772363.3798326
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038089617?origin=resultslist
AB  - LLM-based multi-robot systems (MRS) can take a user's high-level goal and generate coordinated robot actions for spatial tasks. However, human-in-the-loop is still needed when the system benefits from humans providing clarifications or preferences, especially in settings where full autonomy is not yet reliable and the environment is highly uncertain. Motivated by this gap, we conducted a qualitative study (N = 12) in a VR-based multi-robot simulation environment to examine how users construct prompts for MRS and what challenges they perceive during task decomposition, allocation, and execution. Results show that users use prompts to specify robot roles, order, and end goals, and re-prompt when results are inefficient, or execution is not observable. We also found that gaps in workflow visibility, interface support, and coordination logic hinder users' understanding and their ability to intervene. We provide design implications for human-in-the-loop interfaces and prompt support in LLM-based MRS. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
